From: halt95 <halt95@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 09:20:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 54/76] v2-ppfix2: index the persistent per-req-slot block
 table from the align post-process

The PP-deferred Mamba align post-process reads a block table that belongs to a
different step's batch.

Mechanism. There is exactly one gathered destination buffer per KV group,
allocated once and rewritten in place every step
(block_table.py:74-76, :149-176); the Mamba context captures its data_ptr once
(mamba_utils.py:1078-1092) and the kernel takes its row with
`bt_row_idx = batch_idx` (mamba_utils.py:481). But `update_pp_decode_requests`
is the FIRST statement of `execute_model` (model_runner.py:1644), while that
step's `gather_block_tables` runs ~280 lines later (model_runner.py:1442 ->
block_table.py:149). So when `get_prev_sampled_outputs`
(pp_utils.py:121-180) -> `postprocess_sampled` (model_runner.py:1579) ->
`MambaHybridModelState.postprocess_state` (mamba_hybrid.py:386) ->
`run_fused_postprocess_align` (mamba_utils.py:1264) launches the kernel, the
buffer still holds the gather of step T-1, while `idx_mapping` came off a
`PendingRecv` captured at step T-2 (pp_utils.py:57-58, 258-271). Every
per-request value (num_accepted, mamba_state_idx, new_num_computed) is read at
the correct `req_idx = idx_mapping[batch_idx]`; only the block-table row is
taken at `batch_idx`. Under two-way PP micro-batch alternation T-1 and T-2 are
different micro-batches by construction, so the row is a different request's.

Window. The kernel returns before touching the table unless `needs_copy`
(mamba_utils.py:462-465), i.e. `new_num_computed mod mamba_block_size <
num_accepted` - one 3,200-token block crossing inside the accepted window - and
again on `src == dst && bias == 0` (:478-479). Measured on the Arm D 317 boot:
14,610 deferred steps, 10,489 (71.8%) with a row-identity mismatch, but only
~12 boundary crossings across the whole 19-request run. So the hazard is real
and rare: a third-party request's recurrent state is overwritten at the
perpetrator's logical columns a handful of times per boot, which is compatible
with coherent outputs and a passing quality battery.

Fix. Capture the persistent per-req-slot tables
(`BlockTables.block_tables[i].gpu`, block_table.py:53-59 - already the gather
kernel's source, pointers at block_table.py:94-96) in a second int64 pointer
tensor on `MambaSpecDecodeGPUContext`, add `BT_ROW_IS_REQ: tl.constexpr` so
mamba_utils.py:481 becomes `bt_row_idx = req_idx`, and have
`run_fused_postprocess_align` pass that set. `input_block_tables` is
`torch.zeros_like` of the persistent tables, so stride(0) is identical and
`block_table_stride_req` is unchanged (asserted at populate time). `req_idx` is
always in [0, max_num_reqs) and always addresses a row that belongs to the
request, in this step or any later one; recycled slots are already masked to -1
upstream (pp_utils.py:139-140) and skipped (mamba_utils.py:441-442), and that
guard is expressed in req-slot terms, which is now the same space as the row.
The change is correct on the same-step (last-rank / no-PP) path too, so it is
unconditional.

NOT changed: `precopy_mamba_align_fused_kernel` (mamba_utils.py:613-615) keeps
the gathered tables and `batch_idx`. It runs from `preprocess_state`
(mamba_hybrid.py:224-230) five lines after the gather that feeds it
(model_runner.py:1718 -> :1723), so batch row i IS this request there;
`bt_row_idx = req_idx` would break it. Likewise `compute_aligned_state_indices`
(mamba_utils.py:1095-1113) reads the same-step table and is untouched. Do not
port zebgop patch 0010's second hunk.

No `0 <= block_id < num_blocks` guard: the context holds neither the per-group
physical block count nor the per-request `num_blocks`, so adding one is new
plumbing, not a free line. Proposed separately.

Plumbing: `ModelState.set_block_tables` (no-op by default) so the runner can
hand the model state its `BlockTables` (model_runner.py, after construction);
`MambaHybridModelState._ensure_align_ctx` passes the per-group persistent
tensors alongside the gathered ones. When the hook is absent the context logs
once and falls back to the old behaviour rather than crashing.

Test: tests/v1/worker/gpu/test_pp_deferred_mamba_block_table.py - CPU model of
the row selection. A port of `_gather_block_tables_kernel` (with its own
instrument check) fills the shared buffer at T-2 with [r0, r1, r2] and again at
T-1 with [r2]; the production plumbing then runs and the launch is captured
instead of dispatched; the kernel's index arithmetic is replayed against the
recorded arguments, bound to the real kernel signature. r0's deferred copy must
resolve to block ids 10/11 (r0's persistent row). On v2-rc1 it resolves to
30/31 - r2's row, which the T-1 gather left at batch row 0. A second test
asserts the pre-copy launcher still uses the gathered set and batch rows.

Test runs (CPU, the reference host):
  tests/v1/worker/gpu ................................ 4 passed
  tests/v1/ple_offload ............................... 138 passed, 1 skipped
  tests/v1/worker + gpu -k "pp or mamba" ............. 131 passed, 4 failed
All four failures are pre-existing. Re-run serially,
test_profile_cudagraph_memory_frees_throwaway_pool passes (its batch failure
was EADDRINUSE on the hard-coded port 12345), and the other three fail
identically on the v2-rc1 tree: test_hybrid_attention_mamba_tensor_shapes and
